#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078213 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "c02d2000-3fef-59e4-9127-84500ef83eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-418" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n\\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "c7a90d29-e8b9-5a06-94f7-776eb254cc52" :question/math-academy-id "e-419" :question/problem "Given that ${\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54 },$ what is the value of\n${\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg( \\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x }?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{6}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{0}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 54+x\\,{|}_{6}^{0} \\\\  & =18+(0-6) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078218 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "a85f51c5-601b-52ec-82dd-0b611842d3d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-420" :question/problem "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big( 2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+18={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}(2f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{10}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(12)+x\\,{|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =24+(10-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078227 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "3d925b89-dc4d-59e0-bf00-3a3ca90b5169" :question/math-academy-id "e-421" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}$ \nand ${\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}.$  What is the value of ${\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the first given integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}2\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dad3368-3b1f-5f30-adb6-5b54b1058bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287225} {:answer/id #uuid "d454aabc-56ca-5c64-970a-a6847e824c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287226} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c046d-29e7-576c-bd63-b007a849dacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287227} {:answer/id #uuid "b5880e00-93d8-50ca-b06b-d51e42517cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287228} {:answer/id #uuid "2b23a4e3-3cfe-5e99-bd3a-33291a12abb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c046d-29e7-576c-bd63-b007a849dacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c48bd3e-22c1-5da5-929b-92235c89e5ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700eca56-7546-5c46-a5c9-5b46500fdf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-114143" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ 3{\\int }_{8}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ -3{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d39061c-5998-5f0a-97a0-67a9eacaec72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d39061c-5998-5f0a-97a0-67a9eacaec72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298355} :answer-field/id #uuid "1812e228-52dc-5fe2-81f3-feb08a610fa5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a01d0d55-496a-59f0-876c-6cb1db4e698e" :question/math-academy-id "q-345634" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{0}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{0}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7fbd40-a307-5e2a-9ca7-da314561c49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7fbd40-a307-5e2a-9ca7-da314561c49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298358} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2e93b8-c705-5690-ac25-ecf35f01f174" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a789764d-6c23-5081-bf29-e1cefd94e0a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-346059" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5x-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{-3}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-3}^{0}\\,-\\,(-6) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{0-9}{2}\\,+\\,6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{2}\\,+\\,6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3a01c27-b258-5f2f-8181-05b3000f48a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298365} {:answer/id #uuid "522d036b-4e1a-5bf6-94e7-e1a343f49cc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a01c27-b258-5f2f-8181-05b3000f48a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298365} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c7b002-864b-5b0e-b82d-79cd606e879a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "443dc647-fc7d-5181-add0-610b67501f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346181" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{113}{9},$ calculate ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{113}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,8{\\int }_{-3}^{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{113}{9})\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-3}^{6} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,108 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01080b51-c0d9-5a7d-9e36-5bc67d4b4cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01080b51-c0d9-5a7d-9e36-5bc67d4b4cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298372} :answer-field/id #uuid "55dc33ab-63c5-507f-9f01-dd46c490a9e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abacc09f-e24f-5083-9902-ea5b1af36c84" :question/math-academy-id "q-346187" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{6}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=11,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n(-1)+11={\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=10.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{10}(6-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{4}^{10}1\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{4}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot x{|}_{4}^{10}-3(10) \\\\  & =6(10-4)-30 \\\\  & =36-30 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad292ea9-dd5f-5514-b897-9d26801459df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298375} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4e0dc-2414-5dbd-a02c-4ed071f332f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298376} {:answer/id #uuid "ee03515b-63c9-5a57-a099-0a91174bb038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298377} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c8eb6d-129d-5988-aaeb-86043df1b923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298378} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3b158d-7679-5c83-ba2f-9c82989ff367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3b158d-7679-5c83-ba2f-9c82989ff367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298379} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d599d0-48dd-5bd4-bca8-58ce8dd874e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e39e611c-2e6a-5118-851f-9558e33dfe9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4726" :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18,{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the adjacent intervals rule and substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the sum rule for definite integrals and substituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 18-10 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 4 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e8904f-7cbe-5fcf-8a34-e9861f46e3f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298389} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a1b409-1a13-52dc-93b6-19a7061f9933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298390} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ee562e-1a00-517d-81a4-7041ea4d844f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298391} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d95299-ac10-5eb9-b5f6-19067958467c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298392} {:answer/id #uuid "5a48057a-c503-527e-b209-24eb83285ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a48057a-c503-527e-b209-24eb83285ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298393} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ae0418-fa3d-59f6-8cd8-b0a00150d751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f7e1e9b-4799-51a0-a138-eeebd44e608b" :question/math-academy-id "q-4795" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ and ${\\int }_{2}^{0}2f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{0}2f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-2)+8 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34252ab6-fa19-5895-a0e0-0dc3a26dfb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298403} {:answer/id #uuid "4c340bc5-4510-59f3-945b-72aaa54dacb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298404} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6b8493-9750-5e2b-b79e-f0a945888ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298405} {:answer/id #uuid "2b81ae64-1626-5219-922b-3aab9508aef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298406} {:answer/id #uuid "f2db9976-3a9a-5eaf-921f-70211aaea110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c340bc5-4510-59f3-945b-72aaa54dacb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298404} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a38726-a848-5322-86a1-54290e3fc398" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b061a43c-1108-5502-a0d5-189a5c9a0b07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73350" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{6}(3f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+\\frac{1}{3}={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{16}{3}={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(3f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{16}{3})+3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =16+3(6-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
